select public.fd_ai_upsert_chunks('fd-textbook', '825773c58568', 9, $aim_json$[{"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:9.0#1", "source_key": "fd-textbook:9.0", "chapter": 9, "section": "9.0", "heading": "Decide first", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 1, "title": "Chapter 9, Trading Strategies with Options — 9.0 Decide first", "body": "⚙ Decision — choose first; the theory follows\nWhich position profits from a large movement in either direction?\nNorthwind Components will announce the outcome of a major contract tender within three months. An investor believes that the share price, now $28, will move sharply when the result is known — upward if the contract is won, downward if it is lost — and has no view as to which. Three-month options with a strike of $28 cost $2.34 for the call and $1.99 for the put; the call with a strike of $30 costs $1.52. Select the position that corresponds to this view.\nA share allows an investor to express one view: that the price will rise or, by a short sale, that it will fall. Options allow views on the size of a movement independently of its direction, views that the price will remain within a range, and views bounded on one side or both. Each view corresponds to a shape of profit diagram, and each shape can be assembled from calls and puts. This chapter is a catalogue of the principal shapes."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:9.1#2", "source_key": "fd-textbook:9.1", "chapter": 9, "section": "9.1", "heading": "Method and data", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 2, "title": "Chapter 9, Trading Strategies with Options — 9.1 Method and data", "body": "The profit of a position at the expiration of its options is the sum of the profits of its components, computed as in Section 1.5. As there, the interest that could be earned on premiums between the purchase and the expiration is ignored, and all options are European with the same expiration date unless otherwise stated. For any position, four quantities should be computed:\nthe net cost: premiums and share prices paid, less premiums received;\nthe maximum gain and the maximum loss, over all possible share prices at expiration;\nthe breakeven price or prices: the share prices at which the profit is zero.\nBecause the profit of every component is a straight line between strike prices, the profit of the position is also a straight line between strikes, and it is sufficient to compute it at each strike and to note its slope beyond the lowest and highest strikes.\nAll the examples of this chapter use the prices of Table 9.1 for three-month options on Northwind shares, which trade at $28. The prices are consistent with one another and with put–call parity at a risk-free rate of 5 percent.\nTable 9.1 Prices of three-month European options on Northwind shares (share price $28)"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:9.1#3", "source_key": "fd-textbook:9.1", "chapter": 9, "section": "9.1", "heading": "Method and data", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 3, "title": "Chapter 9, Trading Strategies with Options — 9.1 Method and data", "body": "Strike$24$26$28$30$32\nCall$4.84$3.45$2.34$1.52$0.94\nPut$0.55$1.12$1.99$3.14$4.55"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:9.2#4", "source_key": "fd-textbook:9.2", "chapter": 9, "section": "9.2", "heading": "Covered call", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 4, "title": "Chapter 9, Trading Strategies with Options — 9.2 Covered call", "body": "A covered call consists of a long position in the share and a short position in a call. The share covers the writer against the obligation to deliver if the call is exercised. With the $30 call written at $1.52, the net cost is \\(28.00-1.52=\\$26.48\\). If the share price at expiration is above $30, the call is exercised and the share is sold for $30, for a profit of \\(30-26.48=\\$3.52\\), which is the maximum. If it is below $30, the investor keeps the share and the premium; the breakeven price is $26.48, and the maximum loss, if the share becomes worthless, is $26.48.\nThe writer of a covered call gives up the share's gains above the strike in exchange for the premium. The position is appropriate for an investor who intends to hold the share, expects its price to change little, and is prepared to sell at the strike."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:9.2#5", "source_key": "fd-textbook:9.2", "chapter": 9, "section": "9.2", "heading": "Protective put", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 5, "title": "Chapter 9, Trading Strategies with Options — 9.2 Protective put", "body": "A protective put consists of a long position in the share and a long position in a put, as in the hedging example of Section 1.7. With the $26 put bought at $1.12, the net cost is \\(28.00+1.12=\\$29.12\\). If the share price is below $26 at expiration, the put is exercised and the share is sold for $26; the loss is \\(29.12-26=\\$3.12\\), which is the maximum. Above $26 the investor keeps the share. The breakeven price is $29.12, and the gain is unlimited."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:9.2#6", "source_key": "fd-textbook:9.2", "chapter": 9, "section": "9.2", "heading": "The relation to single options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 6, "title": "Chapter 9, Trading Strategies with Options — 9.2 The relation to single options", "body": "The profit diagram of a covered call has the shape of a written put, and that of a protective put has the shape of a purchased call. Put–call parity explains why. From Result 8.2,\n\\[ S_0-c=Ke^{-rT}-p \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad S_0+p=c+Ke^{-rT}. \\]\nThe first states that a share with a written call is equivalent to a written put together with an investment of \\(Ke^{-rT}\\) at the risk-free rate; the second, that a share with a purchased put is equivalent to a purchased call together with the same investment. The reverse positions follow in the same way: a short position in the share combined with a purchased call has the shape of a purchased put, and a short position in the share combined with a written put has the shape of a written call."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:9.2#7", "source_key": "fd-textbook:9.2", "chapter": 9, "section": "9.2", "heading": "Principal-protected notes", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 7, "title": "Chapter 9, Trading Strategies with Options — 9.2 Principal-protected notes", "body": "The second identity is the basis of a product widely sold to retail investors. A principal-protected note returns the investor's principal at maturity whatever happens, together with a share of any rise in an index. A bank can construct it from a zero-coupon bond and a call option. On a three-year note of $1,000 with a risk-free rate of 5 percent, the bond that returns $1,000 at maturity costs \\(1{,}000e^{-0.05\\times3}=\\$860.71\\), leaving $139.29 with which to buy call options on the index and to cover the bank's costs and profit. The investor is protected against a fall in the index and gives up, in exchange, the interest that the $1,000 would have earned.\nConcept check 9.1\nUsing Table 9.1, an investor buys a Northwind share at $28 and writes the call with a strike of $28 for $2.34. What are the maximum gain and the breakeven price?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:9.3#8", "source_key": "fd-textbook:9.3", "chapter": 9, "section": "9.3", "heading": "Spreads", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 8, "title": "Chapter 9, Trading Strategies with Options — 9.3 Spreads", "body": "A spread is a position in two or more options of the same type: all calls or all puts."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:9.3#9", "source_key": "fd-textbook:9.3", "chapter": 9, "section": "9.3", "heading": "Bull spread", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 9, "title": "Chapter 9, Trading Strategies with Options — 9.3 Bull spread", "body": "A bull spread is created by buying a call with strike \\(K_1\\) and writing a call with a higher strike \\(K_2\\). Since a call with a lower strike is the more valuable, the position requires an initial outlay. Buying the $26 call and writing the $30 call costs \\(3.45-1.52=\\$1.93\\). At expiration the position is worth nothing if the share price is below $26, \\(S_T-26\\) between the strikes, and $4 above $30. The maximum loss is the cost, $1.93; the maximum gain is \\(4-1.93=\\$2.07\\); and the breakeven price is \\(26+1.93=\\$27.93\\).\nA bull spread limits both the gain and the loss. An investor who expects a moderate rise gives up the gains above \\(K_2\\), which are thought unlikely, and reduces the cost of the position by the premium of the call written. A bull spread can also be created from puts, by buying the put with the lower strike and writing the put with the higher strike; it then produces an initial receipt."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:9.3#10", "source_key": "fd-textbook:9.3", "chapter": 9, "section": "9.3", "heading": "Bear spread", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 10, "title": "Chapter 9, Trading Strategies with Options — 9.3 Bear spread", "body": "A bear spread is created by buying a put with strike \\(K_2\\) and writing a put with a lower strike \\(K_1\\). Buying the $30 put and writing the $26 put costs \\(3.14-1.12=\\$2.02\\). The position is worth $4 if the share price is below $26, \\(30-S_T\\) between the strikes, and nothing above $30. The maximum gain is \\(4-2.02=\\$1.98\\), the maximum loss $2.02, and the breakeven price \\(30-2.02=\\$27.98\\). It is the position of an investor who expects a moderate fall."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:9.3#11", "source_key": "fd-textbook:9.3", "chapter": 9, "section": "9.3", "heading": "Box spread", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 11, "title": "Chapter 9, Trading Strategies with Options — 9.3 Box spread", "body": "A box spread combines a bull spread made from calls with a bear spread made from puts at the same two strikes. Its payoff is \\(K_2-K_1\\) whatever the share price, so that it is a risk-free investment and must cost \\((K_2-K_1)e^{-rT}\\). The bull and bear spreads above together cost \\(1.93+2.02=\\$3.95\\), and \\(4e^{-0.05\\times0.25}=\\$3.95\\). A box spread whose price differs from this value is an arbitrage, but only if the options are European; with American options the written legs may be exercised early."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:9.3#12", "source_key": "fd-textbook:9.3", "chapter": 9, "section": "9.3", "heading": "Butterfly spread", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 12, "title": "Chapter 9, Trading Strategies with Options — 9.3 Butterfly spread", "body": "A butterfly spread uses three strikes, \\(K_1<K_2<K_3\\), with \\(K_2\\) midway between the others: buy one call at \\(K_1\\), buy one call at \\(K_3\\), and write two calls at \\(K_2\\). With strikes of $24, $28, and $32 the cost is \\(4.84+0.94-2\\times2.34=\\$1.10\\). The position is worth nothing if the share price at expiration is below $24 or above $32, and reaches its greatest value, $4, at $28. The maximum gain is \\(4-1.10=\\$2.90\\); the maximum loss is the cost, $1.10; and the breakeven prices are \\(24+1.10=\\$25.10\\) and \\(32-1.10=\\$30.90\\).\nA butterfly spread is the position of an investor who expects the share price to remain close to \\(K_2\\). It costs little and loses little if the expectation is wrong. The same position can be built from puts, at the same cost: with the puts of Table 9.1, \\(0.55+4.55-2\\times1.99=\\$1.12\\), which differs from $1.10 only through the rounding of the prices."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:9.3#13", "source_key": "fd-textbook:9.3", "chapter": 9, "section": "9.3", "heading": "Calendar spread", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 13, "title": "Chapter 9, Trading Strategies with Options — 9.3 Calendar spread", "body": "In the spreads above, all options expire on the same date. A calendar spread uses options with the same strike and different expirations: write a call with a short life and buy a call with a longer life. Because the short-dated option loses time value more quickly, the position gains if the share price remains near the strike until the first expiration. Its profit at that date is a curve and not a set of straight lines, since the longer option still has time value, and it requires the valuation methods of Chapter 12."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:9.3#14", "source_key": "fd-textbook:9.3", "chapter": 9, "section": "9.3", "heading": "Calendar spread", "kind": "example", "ord": 14, "title": "Chapter 9, Trading Strategies with Options — 9.3 Calendar spread", "body": "Worked example 9.1 — Profit of a butterfly spread\nUsing Table 9.1, compute the profit of the butterfly spread with call strikes of $24, $28, and $32 at share prices of $22, $26, $28, $31, and $35.\n1\nCost. \\(4.84-2\\times2.34+0.94=\\$1.10\\).\n2\n\\(S_T=22\\). All calls expire unexercised. Profit: \\(0-1.10=-\\$1.10\\).\n3\n\\(S_T=26\\). The $24 call pays 2. Profit: \\(2-1.10=\\$0.90\\).\n4\n\\(S_T=28\\). The $24 call pays 4; the others pay nothing. Profit: \\(4-1.10=\\$2.90\\).\n5\n\\(S_T=31\\). The $24 call pays 7; the two written $28 calls cost \\(2\\times3=6\\). Profit: \\(7-6-1.10=-\\$0.10\\).\n6\n\\(S_T=35\\). Payoffs: \\(11-2\\times7+3=0\\). Profit: \\(-\\$1.10\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:9.4#15", "source_key": "fd-textbook:9.4", "chapter": 9, "section": "9.4", "heading": "Combinations", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 15, "title": "Chapter 9, Trading Strategies with Options — 9.4 Combinations", "body": "A combination is a position that includes both calls and puts on the same share."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:9.4#16", "source_key": "fd-textbook:9.4", "chapter": 9, "section": "9.4", "heading": "Straddle", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 16, "title": "Chapter 9, Trading Strategies with Options — 9.4 Straddle", "body": "A straddle is created by buying a call and a put with the same strike and expiration. It is the position selected in the opening exercise. With a strike of $28 the cost is \\(2.34+1.99=\\$4.33\\). At expiration the position is worth \\(|S_T-28|\\). The maximum loss is the cost, incurred if the share price is exactly $28; the breakeven prices are \\(28-4.33=\\$23.67\\) and \\(28+4.33=\\$32.33\\); and the gain is unlimited on the upside and reaches $23.67 if the share becomes worthless.\nA straddle profits from a large movement in either direction. Whether it is a sound investment depends on whether the movement the investor expects is larger than the movement already reflected in option prices. If the market as a whole expects a large movement, the options will be expensive, and the breakeven prices will be far apart. The straddle is therefore a position on volatility: it profits when the realized movement exceeds the movement implied by the prices paid. The reverse position, a written straddle, profits when the share price remains near the strike and carries an unlimited potential loss."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:9.4#17", "source_key": "fd-textbook:9.4", "chapter": 9, "section": "9.4", "heading": "Strangle", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 17, "title": "Chapter 9, Trading Strategies with Options — 9.4 Strangle", "body": "A strangle is created by buying a put with strike \\(K_1\\) and a call with a higher strike \\(K_2\\). Buying the $26 put and the $30 call costs \\(1.12+1.52=\\$2.64\\). The position is worth nothing between the strikes; its breakeven prices are \\(26-2.64=\\$23.36\\) and \\(30+2.64=\\$32.64\\). A strangle is cheaper than a straddle and loses less if the share price is unchanged, but the price must move further before it shows a profit."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:9.4#18", "source_key": "fd-textbook:9.4", "chapter": 9, "section": "9.4", "heading": "Strips and straps", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 18, "title": "Chapter 9, Trading Strategies with Options — 9.4 Strips and straps", "body": "A strip consists of one call and two puts with the same strike and expiration, and a strap of two calls and one put. Both profit from a large movement in either direction. In a strip the investor considers a fall the more likely and gains twice as much per dollar of fall as per dollar of rise; in a strap the emphasis is reversed. With a strike of $28 a strip costs \\(2.34+2\\times1.99=\\$6.32\\) and a strap \\(2\\times2.34+1.99=\\$6.67\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:9.4#19", "source_key": "fd-textbook:9.4", "chapter": 9, "section": "9.4", "heading": "Collar", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 19, "title": "Chapter 9, Trading Strategies with Options — 9.4 Collar", "body": "A collar is a share held together with a purchased put at a strike \\(K_1\\) below the current price and a written call at a strike \\(K_2\\) above it. The put sets a minimum on the value of the holding and the call a maximum, and the premium received for the call pays for part or all of the put. With the $26 put and the $30 call the net cost is \\(28.00+1.12-1.52=\\$27.60\\); the holding will be worth between $26 and $30 at expiration, so that the maximum loss is $1.60 and the maximum gain $2.40. A collar in which the two premiums are equal is a zero-cost collar. Collars are widely used by executives and founders who hold large positions in a single share and wish to limit their risk without selling.\nTable 9.2 Summary of strategies, using the prices of Table 9.1 (per share)\nStrategyConstructionNet costMaximum gainMaximum lossBreakevenView\nCovered callShare; write $30 call$26.48$3.52$26.48$26.48Little change\nProtective putShare; buy $26 put$29.12Unlimited$3.12$29.12Rise, with insurance\nBull spreadBuy $26 call; write $30 call$1.93$2.07$1.93$27.93Moderate rise\nBear spreadBuy $30 put; write $26 put$2.02$1.98$2.02$27.98Moderate fall"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:9.4#20", "source_key": "fd-textbook:9.4", "chapter": 9, "section": "9.4", "heading": "Collar", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 20, "title": "Chapter 9, Trading Strategies with Options — 9.4 Collar", "body": "ButterflyBuy $24 and $32 calls; write two $28 calls$1.10$2.90$1.10$25.10; $30.90Price stays near $28\nStraddleBuy $28 call and $28 put$4.33Unlimited$4.33$23.67; $32.33Large move, either way\nStrangleBuy $26 put and $30 call$2.64Unlimited$2.64$23.36; $32.64Very large move, either way\nCollarShare; buy $26 put; write $30 call$27.60$2.40$1.60$27.60Hold, within limits"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:9.4#21", "source_key": "fd-textbook:9.4", "chapter": 9, "section": "9.4", "heading": "Collar", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 21, "title": "Chapter 9, Trading Strategies with Options — 9.4 Collar", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 9.1 — Profit diagrams of ten strategies\nEach view of the share price has a shape, and each shape a construction\nSelect a strategy. The figure shows its profit at expiration, and the text below gives its construction from the options of Table 9.1, its cost, its maximum gain and loss, and its breakeven prices. Before selecting each, sketch the diagram from the construction and compare."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:9.4#22", "source_key": "fd-textbook:9.4", "chapter": 9, "section": "9.4", "heading": "Collar", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 22, "title": "Chapter 9, Trading Strategies with Options — 9.4 Collar", "body": "Concept check 9.2\nAn investor expects Northwind's share price to remain between $27 and $29 for the next three months and wishes to risk as little as possible. Which strategy of Table 9.2 is most suitable?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:9.5#23", "source_key": "fd-textbook:9.5", "chapter": 9, "section": "9.5", "heading": "The value of a strategy before expiration", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 23, "title": "Chapter 9, Trading Strategies with Options — 9.5 The value of a strategy before expiration", "body": "A profit diagram describes a position at a single date. Before that date the position has a market value equal to the sum of the values of its options, each of which exceeds or falls short of its payoff by its time value. Interactive figure 9.2 shows the value of three strategies as surfaces over the share price and the time remaining, computed from the Black–Scholes–Merton model with the volatility of 39 percent that underlies Table 9.1.\nThe figure makes visible a property that the profit diagrams conceal. A purchased straddle loses value with the passage of time at every share price: its holder pays, day by day, for the possibility of a large movement. A butterfly spread near its middle strike gains value with the passage of time: its holder is paid for bearing the risk of such a movement. A bull spread gains from the passage of time when the share price is near or above its higher strike and loses when the price is near or below its lower strike. The rate at which a position gains or loses value as time passes is its theta, and the positions of this chapter are examined again from this standpoint in Chapter 14."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:9.5#24", "source_key": "fd-textbook:9.5", "chapter": 9, "section": "9.5", "heading": "The value of a strategy before expiration", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 24, "title": "Chapter 9, Trading Strategies with Options — 9.5 The value of a strategy before expiration", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 9.2 — Three-dimensional view\nThe value of a strategy as a function of the share price and the time to expiration\nThe dark line at the front edge is the payoff at expiration; the gold curve is the value at the selected time. Drag to rotate; scroll or pinch to zoom. Move the time slider toward zero with the share price at $28 and compare the three strategies.\nTable equivalent of this figure"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:9.6#25", "source_key": "fd-textbook:9.6", "chapter": 9, "section": "9.6", "heading": "Other payoffs", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 25, "title": "Chapter 9, Trading Strategies with Options — 9.6 Other payoffs", "body": "The strategies of this chapter are those in common use, and they do not exhaust the possibilities. If European options were available at every strike price, any payoff that depends only on the share price at expiration could be constructed from them. A butterfly spread with strikes very close together pays a small amount if the share price finishes in a narrow interval and nothing otherwise; a sufficiently large number of such spreads, in suitable quantities, approximates any payoff function as closely as desired. This observation, which is due to Douglas Breeden and Robert Litzenberger, has an important consequence examined in Chapter 15: the prices of butterfly spreads at different strikes reveal the probabilities that the market assigns to different future share prices.\nConcept check 9.3\nA straddle with a strike of $50 costs $9. On the day before a company's results, an investor who expects “a large movement” of about $6 in the share price, in one direction or the other, considers buying it. What should the investor conclude?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:9.6#26", "source_key": "fd-textbook:9.6", "chapter": 9, "section": "9.6", "heading": "Other payoffs", "kind": "notes", "ord": 26, "title": "Chapter 9, Trading Strategies with Options — 9.6 Other payoffs", "body": "Historical note\nThe vocabulary of this chapter is older than the listed options market. Straddles and spreads were traded in the over-the-counter market in puts and calls in New York and London in the nineteenth century; the terms appear in dealers' handbooks of the period, and the word straddle was in use on the New York Stock Exchange by the 1870s.\nThe best-known loss from the positions of this chapter is that of Barings Bank in 1995, listed in Table 1.3. Its trader in Singapore had written straddles on the Nikkei 225 index in large quantity, collecting premiums on the expectation that the index would remain within a narrow range. After the earthquake at Kobe in January 1995 the index fell sharply, and the trader's attempts to support it by buying futures multiplied the loss. A written straddle earns a small and regular income in exchange for an exposure to large movements that is, in principle, unlimited."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:9.summary#27", "source_key": "fd-textbook:9.summary", "chapter": 9, "section": "9.summary", "heading": "Summary", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 27, "title": "Chapter 9, Trading Strategies with Options — 9.summary Summary", "body": "The profit of any position in shares, calls, and puts is the sum of the profits of its components and is linear between strike prices.\nA covered call has the shape of a written put and a protective put the shape of a purchased call, as put–call parity requires.\nBull and bear spreads limit both gain and loss and express a view of a moderate rise or fall. A box spread is risk-free. A butterfly spread profits when the price remains near its middle strike.\nStraddles, strangles, strips, and straps profit from large movements in either direction; they are positions on volatility, and profit only if the movement exceeds what option prices already reflect.\nA collar confines the value of a shareholding between two strikes, the written call paying for the purchased put.\nBefore expiration, positions that have bought options lose value with the passage of time, and positions that have written them gain."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:9.exercises#28", "source_key": "fd-textbook:9.exercises", "chapter": 9, "section": "9.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 28, "title": "Chapter 9, Trading Strategies with Options — 9.exercises Exercises", "body": "Exercises 9.1 to 9.6 use the prices of Table 9.1.\n9.1 An investor buys the share at $28 and the put with a strike of $28. Find the net cost, the maximum loss, and the breakeven price. Which single option has a profit diagram of the same shape?\nNet cost: \\(28.00+1.99=\\$29.99\\). The holding is worth at least $28, so the maximum loss is $1.99. Breakeven: $29.99. The shape is that of a purchased call with a strike of $28.\n9.2 Construct a bull spread from the calls with strikes of $28 and $32. Find its cost, maximum gain, maximum loss, and breakeven price, and its profit if the share price at expiration is $30.50.\nBuy the $28 call and write the $32 call: cost \\(2.34-0.94=\\$1.40\\). Maximum gain: \\(4-1.40=\\$2.60\\). Maximum loss: $1.40. Breakeven: \\(28+1.40=\\$29.40\\). At $30.50: \\(2.50-1.40=\\$1.10\\).\n9.3 Construct a bull spread from the puts with strikes of $26 and $30. Find the initial cash flow and the maximum gain and loss, and compare the profit diagram with that of the bull spread made from calls with the same strikes."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:9.exercises#29", "source_key": "fd-textbook:9.exercises", "chapter": 9, "section": "9.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 29, "title": "Chapter 9, Trading Strategies with Options — 9.exercises Exercises", "body": "Buy the $26 put and write the $30 put: initial receipt \\(3.14-1.12=\\$2.02\\). Above $30 both expire: gain $2.02. Below $26 the position pays out $4: loss \\(4-2.02=\\$1.98\\). The call spread has a maximum gain of $2.07 and a maximum loss of $1.93. The two diagrams have the same shape and differ by $0.05 at every share price, which is the interest on the difference in initial outlay, \\(1.93+2.02=3.95\\), over three months: \\(3.95(e^{0.0125}-1)=0.05\\).\n9.4 An investor writes the straddle with a strike of $28. Find the range of share prices at expiration over which the position is profitable, and the loss if the share price is $38.\nPremium received: $4.33. The position is profitable between $23.67 and $32.33. At $38 the call is exercised against the writer: \\(4.33-10=-\\$5.67\\).\n9.5 A founder holds Northwind shares worth $28 each and establishes a collar with the $24 put and the $32 call. Find the net cost per share and the range of values of the holding at expiration.\nNet cost of the options: \\(0.55-0.94=-\\$0.39\\), a net receipt. The holding will be worth between $24 and $32 per share; including the receipt, between $24.39 and $32.39."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:9.exercises#30", "source_key": "fd-textbook:9.exercises", "chapter": 9, "section": "9.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 30, "title": "Chapter 9, Trading Strategies with Options — 9.exercises Exercises", "body": "9.6 A dealer offers a box spread with strikes of $24 and $32 for $7.80. Is it correctly priced? What should an investor do?\nThe box pays $8 at expiration with certainty and should cost \\(8e^{-0.0125}=\\$7.90\\). At $7.80 it is cheap: buying it earns \\(\\ln(8/7.80)/0.25=10.1\\) percent per year without risk, against a risk-free rate of 5 percent. From Table 9.1 its cost is \\((4.84-0.94)+(4.55-0.55)=\\$7.90\\), which confirms the fair price.\n9.7 A bank issues a five-year principal-protected note of $10,000 linked to a stock index. The five-year risk-free rate is 4 percent with continuous compounding. How much is available to purchase options and to cover the bank's costs?\nThe zero-coupon bond costs \\(10{,}000e^{-0.20}=\\$8{,}187.31\\). The remainder, $1,812.69, is available for options and costs.\n9.8 Three European calls on a share have strikes of $55, $60, and $65 and cost $10, $7, and $5 respectively. Find the cost of the butterfly spread and the range of share prices at expiration over which it loses money.\nCost: \\(10+5-2\\times7=\\$1\\). The maximum value is $5 at $60. Breakeven prices: \\(55+1=\\$56\\) and \\(65-1=\\$64\\). The spread loses money if the share price is below $56 or above $64."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:9.further-reading#31", "source_key": "fd-textbook:9.further-reading", "chapter": 9, "section": "9.further-reading", "heading": "Further reading", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 31, "title": "Chapter 9, Trading Strategies with Options — 9.further-reading Further reading", "body": "Breeden, D. T., and Litzenberger, R. H. (1978). Prices of state-contingent claims implicit in option prices. Journal of Business, 51(4), 621–651.\nHull, J. C. (2022). Options, Futures, and Other Derivatives (11th ed.). Pearson. Chapter 12.\nMcDonald, R. L. (2013). Derivatives Markets (3rd ed.). Pearson. Chapter 3.\nMcMillan, L. G. (2012). Options as a Strategic Investment (5th ed.). Prentice Hall.\nNatenberg, S. (2015). Option Volatility and Pricing (2nd ed.). McGraw-Hill."}]$aim_json$::jsonb, 'Financial Derivatives (MacroNations textbook)');
